 VALIDATING\x0a SUCCESS / FAILURE\x0a SESSION_ESTABLISHED or LOCKOUT / DENIAL\x0a\x0aAuthentication failures SHALL not reveal sensitive information about the underlying credential state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 9. AUTHENTICATION RATE LIMITING\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support controlled limits on repeated authentication attempts.\x0a\x0aLimits MAY consider:\x0a\x0a- attempts\x3b\x0a- time window\x3b\x0a- session\x3b\x0a- host\x3b\x0a- USB identity\x3b\x0a- recovery state.\x0a\x0aThe implementation SHALL avoid permanent denial caused by simple transient failures unless explicitly designed.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 10. AUTHENTICATION FAILURE\x0a\x0aAuthentication failure SHALL produce a defined state.\x0a\x0aThe system MAY:\x0a\x0a- retry\x3b\x0a- delay\x3b\x0a- lock\x3b\x0a- require additional verification\x3b\x0a- enter recovery\x3b\x0a- require creator intervention.\x0a\x0aFailure handling SHALL not expose secrets or sensitive identity information.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 11. SESSION IDENTITY\x0a\x0aEvery authenticated session SHALL have a unique session identity.\x0a\x0aSession identity SHALL be distinguishable from:\x0a\x0a- creator identity\x3b\x0a- task identity\x3b\x0a- process identity\x3b\x0a- host identity.\x0a\x0aSession IDs SHALL be suitable for correlation across:\x0a\x0a- UI\x3b\x0a- tasks\x3b\x0a- model invocations\x3b\x0a- approvals\x3b\x0a- audit\x3b\x0a- logs\x3b\x0a- security events.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 12. SESSION LIFECYCLE\x0a\x0aSessions SHALL have explicit states.\x0a\x0aAt minimum:\x0a\x0a- CREATED\x0a- AUTHENTICATING\x0a- AUTHENTICATED\x0a- ACTIVE\x0a- IDLE\x0a- LOCKED\x0a- EXPIRED\x0a- REVOKED\x0a- TERMINATED\x0a\x0aState transitions SHALL be authoritative.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 13. SESSION ESTABLISHMENT\x0a\x0aA session SHALL be established only after successful authentication.\x0a\x0aSession establishment SHALL initialize:\x0a\x0a- session identity\x3b\x0a- creator identity reference\x3b\x0a- authorization context\x3b\x0a- creation time\x3b\x0a- expiration policy\x3b\x0a- host context\x3b\x0a- USB context\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- relevant session capabilities.\x0a\x0aSession establishment SHALL NOT copy raw credentials into ordinary session state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 14. SESSION CONTEXT\x0a\x0aEvery privileged operation SHALL have an explicit or derivable session context.\x0a\x0aSession context MAY include:\x0a\x0a- creator identity\x3b\x0a- session identity\x3b\x0a- authorization scope\x3b\x0a- authentication strength\x3b\x0a- creation time\x3b\x0a- expiration\x3b\x0a- host identity\x3b\x0a- USB identity\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- approval context\x3b\x0a- task context.\x0a\x0aSession context SHALL be validated before sensitive actions.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 15. SESSION EXPIRATION\x0a\x0aSessions SHALL support expiration.\x0a\x0aExpiration MAY be based on:\x0a\x0a- inactivity\x3b\x0a- absolute lifetime\x3b\x0a- security event\x3b\x0a- credential change\x3b\x0a- creator logout\x3b\x0a- USB removal\x3b\x0a- host change\x3b\x0a- policy change.\x0a\x0aExpired sessions SHALL not continue to authorize new privileged actions.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 16. IDLE TIMEOUT\x0a\x0aThe final implementation MAY support configurable idle timeout.\x0a\x0aIdle timeout SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- active work\x3b\x0a- voice sessions\x3b\x0a- approvals\x3b\x0a- long-running tasks\x3b\x0a- background work.\x0a\x0aLocking the UI SHALL not necessarily terminate authorized background tasks unless task policy requires it.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 17. SESSION LOCK\x0a\x0aWhen a session is locked:\x0a\x0a- privileged interactive actions SHALL require re-authentication\x3b\x0a- sensitive UI content SHALL be protected\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault access SHALL remain protected\x3b\x0a- pending approvals SHALL be handled according to policy\x3b\x0a- active tasks SHALL follow their own lifecycle.\x0a\x0aLocking SHALL not falsely imply that all background execution has stopped.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 18. LOGOUT\x0a\x0aLogout SHALL invalidate the interactive session.\x0a\x0aLogout SHALL:\x0a\x0a- revoke session authorization\x3b\x0a- clear or protect sensitive session material\x3b\x0a- stop interactive privileged actions\x3b\x0a- preserve task state where appropriate\x3b\x0a- update audit state.\x0a\x0aBackground tasks SHALL continue only if their independent authorization remains valid.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 19. SESSION REVOCATION\x0a\x0aA session SHALL be revocable independently of creator identity.\x0a\x0aRevocation MAY occur because of:\x0a\x0a- creator request\x3b\x0a- security event\x3b\x0a- credential compromise\x3b\x0a- USB removal\x3b\x0a- host compromise\x3b\x0a- policy change\x3b\x0a- administrative recovery\x3b\x0a- detected replay\x3b\x0a- session expiration.\x0a\x0aRevocation SHALL invalidate future privileged use of that session.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 20. AUTHORIZATION CONTEXT\x0a\x0aAuthorization SHALL be evaluated within context.\x0a\x0aContext MAY include:\x0a\x0a- creator\x3b\x0a- session\x3b\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- resource\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- host\x3b\x0a- USB\x3b\x0a- network\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- approval\x3b\x0a- time\x3b\x0a- data classification.\x0a\x0aThe same creator may have different authorization outcomes for different contexts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 21. LEAST PRIVILEGE\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL use least privilege.\x0a\x0aA session SHALL receive only the authority required for its current legitimate purpose.\x0a\x0aCapabilities SHALL not inherit broader authority merely because a creator session exists.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 22. TASK-BOUND AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aWhere practical, privileged operations SHALL be bound to a specific task.\x0a\x0aThis reduces the possibility of a model, tool, or UI action reusing unrelated authorization.\x0a\x0aTask-bound authorization SHALL include:\x0a\x0a- task identity\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- expiration\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- creator/session context.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 23. CAPABILITY-BOUND AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aAuthorization SHALL be evaluated per capability.\x0a\x0aA creator being authenticated SHALL not automatically authorize:\x0a\x0a- shell access\x3b\x0a- filesystem modification\x3b\x0a- network actions\x3b\x0a- browser control\x3b\x0a- credential use\x3b\x0a- repository modification\x3b\x0a- host installation\x3b\x0a- updates\x3b\x0a- recovery\x3b\x0a- self-improvement promotion.\x0a\x0aThose operations remain governed by their respective contracts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 24. APPROVAL-BOUND AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aWhere approval is required, authorization SHALL include the approved operation and relevant scope.\x0a\x0aApproval SHALL not become a general-purpose permission.\x0a\x0aApproval MAY expire after:\x0a\x0a- execution\x3b\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- scope change\x3b\x0a- task cancellation\x3b\x0a- session termination\x3b\x0a- security state change.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 25. PERMISSION CACHE\x0a\x0aLANUX MAY cache authorization decisions where safe.\x0a\x0aCached authorization SHALL include sufficient context to prevent reuse outside:\x0a\x0a- the intended session\x3b\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- time\x3b\x0a- security state.\x0a\x0aCritical permissions SHALL not become permanent merely because they were once approved.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 26. CRITICAL OPERATIONS\x0a\x0aCritical operations SHALL require the strongest applicable authorization.\x0a\x0aExamples include:\x0a\x0a- SecretsVault unlock\x3b\x0a- secret creation\x3b\x0a- secret modification\x3b\x0a- destructive deletion\x3b\x0a- host installation\x3b\x0a- security policy changes\x3b\x0a- production self-improvement promotion\x3b\x0a- recovery operations affecting protected state\x3b\x0a- privileged update activation.\x0a\x0aCritical operations SHALL not rely solely on stale session authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 27. SECRETSVAULT INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aSecretsVault SHALL remain the authoritative secure credential storage mechanism.\x0a\x0aIdentity/session architecture SHALL not create a second credential store.\x0a\x0aVault access SHALL be:\x0a\x0a- session-aware\x3b\x0a- permission-aware\x3b\x0a- task-aware where applicable\x3b\x0a- audited where required\x3b\x0a- minimized.\x0a\x0aRaw secret values SHALL not be copied into ordinary session state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 28. SESSION CREDENTIAL MATERIAL\x0a\x0aSession-related credential material SHALL be minimized.\x0a\x0aWhere temporary tokens or derived credentials exist, they SHALL have:\x0a\x0a- defined scope\x3b\x0a- lifetime\x3b\x0a- storage location\x3b\x0a- revocation behavior\x3b\x0a- exposure boundary.\x0a\x0aExpired credential material SHALL not remain usable.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 29. TOKEN / SESSION MATERIAL\x0a\x0aIf tokens are used, the architecture SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- issuance\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- lifetime\x3b\x0a- rotation\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- integrity\x3b\x0a- replay resistance\x3b\x0a- secure storage\x3b\x0a- secure transmission.\x0a\x0aExact token technology SHALL be determined by the final implementation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 30. REPLAY PROTECTION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL protect sensitive session and authorization operations against replay.\x0a\x0aWhere appropriate, the architecture SHALL use:\x0a\x0a- unique session identifiers\x3b\x0a- nonces\x3b\x0a- timestamps\x3b\x0a- sequence numbers\x3b\x0a- expiration\x3b\x0a- transaction IDs\x3b\x0a- state versions.\x0a\x0aThe final mechanism SHALL be selected based on actual implementation requirements.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 31. STALE AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aAuthorization SHALL become invalid when the underlying state changes materially.\x0a\x0aExamples:\x0a\x0a- session revoked\x3b\x0a- creator logged out\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- task cancelled\x3b\x0a- approval expired\x3b\x0a- capability revoked\x3b\x0a- host state changed\x3b\x0a- USB identity changed\x3b\x0a- policy changed.\x0a\x0aA stale authorization attempt SHALL be rejected or revalidated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 32. USB IDENTITY\x0a\x0aLANUX MAY maintain a USB/media identity separate from creator identity.\x0a\x0aUSB identity MAY help determine:\x0a\x0a- whether this is a known LANUX operational medium\x3b\x0a- state continuity\x3b\x0a- expected configuration\x3b\x0a- integrity\x3b\x0a- recovery context.\x0a\x0aUSB identity SHALL NOT by itself authenticate the creator.\x0a\x0aA copied USB SHALL not automatically become an authenticated creator identity.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 33. USB COPY THREAT\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL assume that physical possession of a USB may not prove creator identity.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- copied USB contents\x3b\x0a- cloned files\x3b\x0a- copied configuration\x3b\x0a- stolen backups\x3b\x0a- extracted metadata.\x0a\x0aSensitive creator authentication SHALL remain independent from portable files alone.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 34. HOST IDENTITY\x0a\x0aLANUX MAY maintain a host identity or fingerprint.\x0a\x0aHost identity SHALL be used only for approved purposes such as:\x0a\x0a- host-specific state\x3b\x0a- dependency state\x3b\x0a- ownership tracking\x3b\x0a- diagnostics\x3b\x0a- security policy\x3b\x0a- recovery.\x0a\x0aHost identity SHALL not silently become creator identity.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 35. HOST CHANGE\x0a\x0aWhen LANUX runs on a different host, the session architecture SHALL require appropriate revalidation.\x0a\x0aHost changes MAY trigger:\x0a\x0a- dependency checks\x3b\x0a- security checks\x3b\x0a- reauthentication\x3b\x0a- permission re-evaluation\x3b\x0a- capability restriction\x3b\x0a- configuration selection.\x0a\x0aThe exact behavior SHALL depend on the final security policy.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 36. GITHUB IDENTITY\x0a\x0aGitHub identity SHALL remain separate from creator identity.\x0a\x0aGitHub authentication SHALL authorize GitHub operations only according to the Network & External Services and Canonical Home Sync contracts.\x0a\x0aA valid GitHub credential SHALL not automatically grant local LANUX administrative access.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 37. EXTERNAL PROVIDER IDENTITY\x0a\x0aExternal provider credentials SHALL be scoped to their provider and intended operations.\x0a\x0aProvider authentication SHALL not automatically grant:\x0a\x0a- LANUX creator access\x3b\x0a- local filesystem access\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault administration\x3b\x0a- task administration.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 38. MODEL IDENTITY\x0a\x0aModel identity SHALL remain separate from creator identity.\x0a\x0aA model cannot authenticate itself as the creator merely because it is operating within an authenticated session.\x0a\x0aModel-generated requests SHALL remain subject to authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 39. TOOL IDENTITY\x0a\x0aEach governed capability/tool SHALL have an identifiable identity.\x0a\x0aTool identity SHALL include, where applicable:\x0a\x0a- tool ID\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- source\x3b\x0a- trust state\x3b\x0a- capability class\x3b\x0a- lifecycle state.\x0a\x0aA trusted tool SHALL still require authorization for its use.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 40. SESSION-TO-TASK RELATIONSHIP\x0a\x0aTasks SHALL record the session that initiated them where appropriate.\x0a\x0aThis relationship SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- audit\x3b\x0a- cancellation\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- visibility\x3b\x0a- recovery.\x0a\x0aLong-running tasks MAY outlive an interactive session only when explicitly permitted.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 41. SESSION-TO-MODEL RELATIONSHIP\x0a\x0aModel invocations MAY carry session context where required for authorization and audit.\x0a\x0aThe model SHALL not inherit unrestricted session authority.\x0a\x0aOnly the minimum authorization context required for the task SHALL be exposed.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 42. SESSION-TO-TOOL RELATIONSHIP\x0a\x0aTool calls SHALL retain sufficient context to determine:\x0a\x0a- initiating session\x3b\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- approval\x3b\x0a- target.\x0a\x0aTool execution SHALL not rely solely on UI state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 43. SESSION-TO-UI RELATIONSHIP\x0a\x0aUI sessions SHALL map to authenticated LANUX sessions.\x0a\x0aThe UI SHALL not be able to fabricate:\x0a\x0a- creator identity\x3b\x0a- session identity\x3b\x0a- authorization state\x3b\x0a- approval state.\x0a\x0aBackend authority SHALL remain authoritative.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 44. MULTI-SESSION ARCHITECTURE\x0a\x0aIf multiple sessions are supported, LANUX SHALL isolate them.\x0a\x0aEach session SHALL have independent:\x0a\x0a- identity\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- session state\x3b\x0a- expiration\x3b\x0a- UI state\x3b\x0a- approval context.\x0a\x0aOne session SHALL not access another session's protected state without explicit authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 45. SESSION PRIORITY\x0a\x0aSessions MAY have priority for interactive resources.\x0a\x0aPriority SHALL not bypass security.\x0a\x0aA high-priority session SHALL not automatically gain broader capability access.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 46. BACKGROUND AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aBackground work SHALL use an explicit authorization basis.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL not rely indefinitely on a creator's interactive session unless policy explicitly allows it.\x0a\x0aBackground authorization MAY be:\x0a\x0a- task-scoped\x3b\x0a- schedule-scoped\x3b\x0a- capability-scoped\x3b\x0a- creator-approved\x3b\x0a- system-defined.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 47. AUTONOMOUS SESSION\x0a\x0aIf LANUX creates an autonomous operational context, that context SHALL remain explicitly distinguishable from the creator's human session.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL contain:\x0a\x0a- originating creator/session\x3b\x0a- purpose\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- lifetime\x3b\x0a- capabilities\x3b\x0a- resource limits\x3b\x0a- cancellation authority.\x0a\x0aAutonomous context SHALL not become unrestricted administrator authority.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 48. SESSION DELEGATION\x0a\x0aA session MAY delegate bounded authority to a child task or capability.\x0a\x0aDelegation SHALL specify:\x0a\x0a- issuer\x3b\x0a- recipient\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- expiration\x3b\x0a- constraints.\x0a\x0aDelegated authority SHALL not exceed the issuer's own authority.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 49. DELEGATION CHAIN\x0a\x0aDelegated authorization SHALL preserve provenance.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL be able to determine:\x0a\x0a- original creator\x3b\x0a- originating session\x3b\x0a- parent task\x3b\x0a- delegated capability\x3b\x0a- downstream task/tool/model.\x0a\x0aDelegation SHALL have bounded depth.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 50. REVOCATION PROPAGATION\x0a\x0aWhen a parent authorization is revoked, dependent delegated authorization SHALL be reevaluated or revoked.\x0a\x0aExamples:\x0a\x0a- creator logout\x3b\x0a- session revocation\x3b\x0a- task cancellation\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- capability revocation.\x0a\x0aRevocation SHALL not stop at the first layer if downstream authority remains dangerous.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 51. SECURITY HOLD\x0a\x0aA security hold SHALL be able to restrict or invalidate active authorization.\x0a\x0aDepending on severity, it MAY:\x0a\x0a- block privileged actions\x3b\x0a- lock sessions\x3b\x0a- revoke temporary credentials\x3b\x0a- stop model-generated actions\x3b\x0a- pause autonomous work\x3b\x0a- require creator reauthentication.\x0a\x0aSecurity hold behavior SHALL be defined by the Security Boundary Contract.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 52. EMERGENCY LOCK\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support an emergency lock state where appropriate.\x0a\x0aEmergency lock SHALL prioritize protection over convenience.\x0a\x0aIt MAY:\x0a\x0a- terminate privileged sessions\x3b\x0a- lock SecretsVault\x3b\x0a- block new tools\x3b\x0a- pause autonomous work\x3b\x0a- require fresh authentication.\x0a\x0aExisting safe shutdown/recovery behavior SHALL remain available where appropriate.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 53. CREDENTIAL ROTATION\x0a\x0aCredentials used by LANUX SHALL support rotation where applicable.\x0a\x0aRotation SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- active sessions\x3b\x0a- running tasks\x3b\x0a- provider connections\x3b\x0a- cached tokens\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- persistence\x3b\x0a- audit.\x0a\x0aOld credential material SHALL cease to authorize operations when rotation requires invalidation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 54. CREDENTIAL COMPROMISE\x0a\x0aIf credential compromise is suspected, LANUX SHALL support controlled response.\x0a\x0aResponse MAY include:\x0a\x0a- session revocation\x3b\x0a- token invalidation\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault lock\x3b\x0a- provider credential rotation\x3b\x0a- GitHub credential revocation\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- audit event\x3b\x0a- recovery.\x0a\x0aThe UI SHALL communicate the security state appropriately.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 55. LOST OR STOLEN USB\x0a\x0aIf the USB is lost or stolen, creator identity SHALL remain protected independently of the USB contents.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- credential revocation\x3b\x0a- session revocation\x3b\x0a- provider credential rotation\x3b\x0a- GitHub credential handling\x3b\x0a- recovery from another trusted medium\x3b\x0a- creation of a replacement operational USB.\x0a\x0aA stolen USB SHALL not automatically provide creator access.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 56. REPLACEMENT USB\x0a\x0aWhen replacing a USB, LANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- new physical medium\x3b\x0a- restored portable state\x3b\x0a- creator identity\x3b\x0a- previous USB identity\x3b\x0a- current security state.\x0a\x0aReplacement SHALL require appropriate authentication and integrity validation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 57. RECOVERY ACCESS\x0a\x0aRecovery access SHALL be explicitly scoped.\x0a\x0aRecovery mechanisms SHALL not become a hidden unrestricted administrative channel.\x0a\x0aRecovery MAY provide access to:\x0a\x0a- diagnostics\x3b\x0a- restore operations\x3b\x0a- creator reauthentication\x3b\x0a- credential rotation\x3b\x0a- safe export/import.\x0a\x0aRecovery authority SHALL be governed separately where necessary.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 58. OFFLINE AUTHENTICATION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support offline creator authentication to the extent required by the final portable architecture.\x0a\x0aOffline authentication SHALL not require unavailable network services unless the selected security architecture explicitly depends on them.\x0a\x0aOffline credentials SHALL be protected against extraction and replay.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 59. NETWORK-DEPENDENT AUTHENTICATION\x0a\x0aWhere authentication depends on an external provider, LANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- authentication unavailable\x3b\x0a- provider unavailable\x3b\x0a- network unavailable\x3b\x0a- credential invalid\x3b\x0a- session expired.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL not convert network failure into credential failure.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 60. AUTHENTICATION DURING USB REMOVAL\x0a\x0aUSB removal SHALL invalidate or suspend session state according to the USB Removal & Safe Shutdown Contract.\x0a\x0aSensitive session material SHALL not remain usable after LANUX-owned files are unavailable.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 61. AUTHENTICATION DURING RECOVERY\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL preserve identity boundaries.\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL not silently authenticate a creator merely because recovery files are present.\x0a\x0aWhere authentication is unavailable, recovery SHALL expose only the explicitly allowed recovery operations.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 62. AUTHENTICATION DURING UPDATE\x0a\x0aUpdates SHALL not silently replace creator identity or session policy.\x0a\x0aAuthentication compatibility SHALL be checked before activating updated identity components.\x0a\x0aFailed updates SHALL not leave an ambiguous identity state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 63. SESSION PERSISTENCE\x0a\x0aSession state SHALL be classified carefully.\x0a\x0aSensitive live authentication material SHALL generally remain temporary unless the final security architecture explicitly permits secure persistence.\x0a\x0aPersistent records MAY include:\x0a\x0a- session metadata\x3b\x0a- audit references\x3b\x0a- task references\x3b\x0a- termination state.\x0a\x0aRaw session secrets SHALL not be persisted in ordinary state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 64. SESSION RECOVERY\x0a\x0aAfter restart, LANUX SHALL determine whether previous sessions:\x0a\x0a- expired\x3b\x0a- terminated\x3b\x0a- were interrupted\x3b\x0a- can safely resume\x3b\x0a- require reauthentication.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL prefer reauthentication over unsafe restoration of privileged state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 65. SESSION MIGRATION\x0a\x0aSession migration across hosts SHALL not be assumed safe.\x0a\x0aIf supported, migration SHALL require:\x0a\x0a- secure state transfer\x3b\x0a- creator authentication\x3b\x0a- integrity validation\x3b\x0a- authorization revalidation\x3b\x0a- host validation.\x0a\x0aA portable USB SHALL not automatically migrate a privileged live session between machines.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 66. ACCESS CONTROL DECISION\x0a\x0aEvery sensitive action SHALL conceptually evaluate:\x0a\x0aIDENTITY\x0a SESSION\x0a AUTHORIZATION\x0a CAPABILITY\x0a TARGET\x0a CONTEXT\x0a SECURITY STATE\x0a APPROVAL\x0a RESOURCE\x0a EXECUTION\x0a\x0aFailure at a required stage SHALL prevent execution.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 67. ACCESS CONTROL CACHE INVALIDATION\x0a\x0aCached access decisions SHALL be invalidated when relevant state changes.\x0a\x0aTriggers MAY include:\x0a\x0a- logout\x3b\x0a- session expiry\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- policy change\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- capability change\x3b\x0a- task cancellation\x3b\x0a- USB change\x3b\x0a- host change.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 68. ACCESS CONTROL FAILURE\x0a\x0aAccess control failures SHALL be classified.\x0a\x0aExamples:\x0a\x0a- unauthenticated\x3b\x0a- session expired\x3b\x0a- permission denied\x3b\x0a- approval required\x3b\x0a- approval expired\x3b\x0a- capability unavailable\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- resource unavailable\x3b\x0a- target unauthorized\x3b\x0a- stale authorization.\x0a\x0aThe UI SHALL communicate the appropriate class without exposing unnecessary security details.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 69. FAIL-CLOSED\x0a\x0aSecurity-sensitive access control SHALL fail closed.\x0a\x0aIf LANUX cannot determine whether a privileged action is authorized, it SHALL not execute the action.\x0a\x0aSafe read-only or diagnostic behavior MAY continue where explicitly permitted.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 70. FAIL-SAFE\x0a\x0aFail-safe behavior MAY permit non-sensitive operations necessary for:\x0a\x0a- shutdown\x3b\x0a- recovery\x3b\x0a- diagnostics\x3b\x0a- integrity preservation.\x0a\x0aFail-safe SHALL never become a route around authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 71. ACCESS CONTROL AND MODELS\x0a\x0aModels SHALL receive only the authorization context necessary for their task.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL not be able to inspect or alter authorization policy unless explicitly authorized to do so.\x0a\x0aModel-generated access requests SHALL be treated as requests.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 72. ACCESS CONTROL AND TOOLS\x0a\x0aTools SHALL execute only under the applicable authorization context.\x0a\x0aTool identity alone SHALL not grant access.\x0a\x0aTools SHALL not retain creator authority beyond the intended invocation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 73. ACCESS CONTROL AND UI\x0a\x0aUI controls SHALL be derived from current authorization state where possible.\x0a\x0aHidden or disabled controls SHALL not be the only security mechanism.\x0a\x0aBackend enforcement SHALL remain authoritative.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 74. ACCESS CONTROL AND MEMORY\x0a\x0aMemory access SHALL be authorization-aware.\x0a\x0aA session SHALL not automatically receive all historical memory.\x0a\x0aMemory access SHALL respect:\x0a\x0a- classification\x3b\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- creator permissions\x3b\x0a- retention\x3b\x0a- privacy.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 75. ACCESS CONTROL AND PROJECTS\x0a\x0aProject-level access SHALL be separately represented where necessary.\x0a\x0aA creator may have different access to:\x0a\x0a- personal state\x3b\x0a- LANUX project state\x3b\x0a- experimental projects\x3b\x0a- recovery state\x3b\x0a- external repositories.\x0a\x0aProject ownership SHALL not override security policy.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 76. ACCESS CONTROL AND REPOSITORIES\x0a\x0aRepository operations SHALL require explicit authorization.\x0a\x0aGitHub authentication SHALL not automatically authorize local repository mutation.\x0a\x0aLocal repository access SHALL remain governed by Sandbox and capability controls.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 77. ACCESS CONTROL AND HOST INSTALLATION\x0a\x0aHost installation SHALL require appropriate authorization and, where applicable, creator approval.\x0a\x0aInstallation authority SHALL be separate from ordinary application operation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 78. ACCESS CONTROL AND SELF-IMPROVEMENT\x0a\x0aSelf-improvement actions SHALL have explicit authorization.\x0a\x0aModels SHALL not self-authorize:\x0a\x0a- production promotion\x3b\x0a- security-policy changes\x3b\x0a- creator identity changes\x3b\x0a- permission expansion\x3b\x0a- unrestricted resource increases.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 79. ACCESS CONTROL AND UPDATES\x0a\x0aUpdate authorization SHALL remain separate from ordinary task authorization.\x0a\x0aProduction updates SHALL use the Update & Upgrade Contract.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 80. ACCESS CONTROL AND RECOVERY\x0a\x0aRecovery actions SHALL use explicit recovery authorization.\x0a\x0aDestructive recovery SHALL require stronger authorization than diagnostic recovery where appropriate.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 81. ACCESS CONTROL AND NETWORK\x0a\x0aExternal network actions SHALL use capability and session authorization.\x0a\x0aRemote provider credentials SHALL remain scoped.\x0a\x0aA network connection SHALL not itself imply authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 82. ACCESS CONTROL AND RESOURCE GOVERNANCE\x0a\x0aResource allocation SHALL be separate from identity.\x0a\x0aAuthentication SHALL not guarantee unlimited:\x0a\x0a- CPU\x3b\x0a- memory\x3b\x0a- GPU\x3b\x0a- storage\x3b\x0a- network\x3b\x0a- model usage.\x0a\x0aResource governance SHALL remain enforceable.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 83. ACCESS CONTROL AND LOGGING\x0a\x0aAccess decisions SHALL generate appropriate audit/log records.\x0a\x0aRecords SHOULD include:\x0a\x0a- session\x3b\x0a- creator reference\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- outcome\x3b\x0a- reason class\x3b\x0a- timestamp\x3b\x0a- correlation ID.\x0a\x0aSecrets SHALL not appear in the record.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 84. ACCESS CONTROL AND DATA HANDLING\x0a\x0aAuthorization SHALL consider data classification.\x0a\x0aMore sensitive data MAY require:\x0a\x0a- stronger authentication\x3b\x0a- narrower scope\x3b\x0a- explicit approval\x3b\x0a- restricted model access\x3b\x0a- restricted external transmission.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 85. ACCESS CONTROL AND CONFIGURATION\x0a\x0aChanges to access-control configuration SHALL be protected.\x0a\x0aA model or ordinary task SHALL not silently modify:\x0a\x0a- creator identity\x3b\x0a- authentication policy\x3b\x0a- permission policy\x3b\x0a- session lifetime\x3b\x0a- critical security settings.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 86. ACCESS CONTROL AND DIAGNOSTICS\x0a\x0aDiagnostics SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- authentication failure\x3b\x0a- authorization failure\x3b\x0a- capability failure\x3b\x0a- session failure\x3b\x0a- policy failure.\x0a\x0aDiagnostic information SHALL not disclose protected credentials or bypass information.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 87. ACCESS CONTROL AND AUDIT\x0a\x0aSecurity-sensitive access events SHALL remain auditable.\x0a\x0aAudit SHALL preserve enough provenance to reconstruct:\x0a\x0a- who\x3b\x0a- which session\x3b\x0a- which task\x3b\x0a- which capability\x3b\x0a- which target\x3b\x0a- what decision\x3b\x0a- what result.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 88. ACCESS CONTROL AND USB COPY\x0a\x0aIf a USB copy is detected or suspected, LANUX SHALL not assume identity continuity.\x0a\x0aIntegrity and identity validation SHALL determine what operations are permitted.\x0a\x0aCreator authentication SHALL remain independently required.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 89. ACCESS CONTROL AND HOST COMPROMISE\x0a\x0aIf the host is suspected to be compromised, LANUX SHALL treat the host as an untrusted environment.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- restricted operation\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- session revocation\x3b\x0a- credential protection\x3b\x0a- safe shutdown\x3b\x0a- recovery.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL not claim protection against a fully compromised host beyond what the platform permits.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 90. ACCESS CONTROL AND PHYSICAL SECURITY\x0a\x0aPhysical possession of:\x0a\x0a- USB\x3b\x0a- PC\x3b\x0a- backups\x3b\x0a- recovery media\x0a\x0ashall not automatically equal creator authorization.\x0a\x0aThe final implementation SHALL identify which security properties can and cannot survive physical compromise.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 91. ACCESS CONTROL STATE MACHINE\x0a\x0aThe overall access state MAY conceptually transition through:\x0a\x0aUNAUTHENTICATED\x0a AUTHENTICATING\x0a AUTHENTICATED\x0a SESSION_ACTIVE\x0a SESSION_LOCKED\x0a SESSION_EXPIRED / SESSION_REVOKED\x0a TERMINATED\x0a\x0aSecurity hold and recovery states SHALL be able to interrupt ordinary session progression.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 92. SESSION CORRELATION\x0a\x0aSession IDs SHALL support correlation across:\x0a\x0a- UI\x3b\x0a- tasks\x3b\x0a- model calls\x3b\x0a- tools\x3b\x0a- approvals\x3b\x0a- logs\x3b\x0a- audits\x3b\x0a- notifications.\x0a\x0aCorrelation identifiers SHALL not contain sensitive secrets.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 93. SESSION TIME\x0a\x0aSession timing SHALL use reliable system time mechanisms appropriate to the platform.\x0a\x0aWhere clock manipulation can affect security, LANUX SHALL consider:\x0a\x0a- monotonic timing\x3b\x0a- expiration validation\x3b\x0a- rollback detection\x3b\x0a- server time where available.\x0a\x0aExact implementation SHALL be determined later.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 94. SESSION CONCURRENCY\x0a\x0aConcurrent sessions SHALL be explicitly supported or explicitly prohibited.\x0a\x0aIf supported, LANUX SHALL define:\x0a\x0a- isolation\x3b\x0a- shared-resource rules\x3b\x0a- conflict resolution\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- task ownership\x3b\x0a- UI visibility.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 95. CREATOR PRESENCE\x0a\x0aWhere sensitive operations require active creator presence, LANUX SHALL define what constitutes presence.\x0a\x0aPresence SHALL not be inferred solely from:\x0a\x0a- USB insertion\x3b\x0a- open UI\x3b\x0a- Windows login\x3b\x0a- model activity.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 96. REAUTHENTICATION\x0a\x0aLANUX MAY require reauthentication for sensitive actions.\x0a\x0aTriggers MAY include:\x0a\x0a- elapsed time\x3b\x0a- sensitive operation\x3b\x0a- security state change\x3b\x0a- host change\x3b\x0a- credential rotation\x3b\x0a- recovery\x3b\x0a- update\x3b\x0a- privilege escalation.\x0a\x0aReauthentication SHALL produce a fresh authorization context.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 97. PRIVILEGE ESCALATION\x0a\x0aPrivilege escalation SHALL be explicit.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL identify:\x0a\x0a- requested privilege\x3b\x0a- requesting session\x3b\x0a- purpose\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- duration\x3b\x0a- approval\x3b\x0a- result.\x0a\x0aPrivilege escalation SHALL not become permanent by default.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 98. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY\x0a\x0aTemporary authority SHALL have:\x0a\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- issuer\x3b\x0a- recipient\x3b\x0a- lifetime\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- audit.\x0a\x0aTemporary authority SHALL expire automatically.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 99. ACCESS CONTROL TESTING\x0a\x0aThe final implementation SHALL test:\x0a\x0a- authentication success/failure\x3b\x0a- session creation\x3b\x0a- session expiration\x3b\x0a- logout\x3b\x0a- lock\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- stale authorization\x3b\x0a- replay\x3b\x0a- USB copy\x3b\x0a- host change\x3b\x0a- provider separation\x3b\x0a- capability separation\x3b\x0a- task-bound authorization\x3b\x0a- approval expiration\x3b\x0a- security hold\x3b\x0a- recovery\x3b\x0a- update\x3b\x0a- offline operation\x3b\x0a- concurrent sessions\x3b\x0a- delegated authority\x3b\x0a- revocation propagation.\x0a\x0aSecurity-sensitive tests SHALL include negative cases.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 100. REQUIREMENTS GATE\x0a\x0aBefore production implementation, LANUX SHALL establish from the completed system:\x0a\x0a- actual creator authentication implementation\x3b\x0a- current session architecture\x3b\x0a- PermissionGateway behavior\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault integration\x3b\x0a- ToolRegistry integration\x3b\x0a- existing approval mechanism\x3b\x0a- existing session/UI mechanisms\x3b\x0a- current identity model\x3b\x0a- current host/USB identity behavior\x3b\x0a- existing tests\x3b\x0a- recovery behavior\x3b\x0a- update behavior\x3b\x0a- offline authentication requirements.\x0a\x0aProduction implementation SHALL begin only after these requirements are verified against the LANUX repository.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 101. IMPLEMENTATION BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aThis contract authorizes architecture definition only.\x0a\x0aIt does NOT authorize:\x0a\x0a- modifying the LANUX development repository\x3b\x0a- changing authentication\x3b\x0a- changing PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- changing SecretsVault\x3b\x0a- changing ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- changing TaskStore\x3b\x0a- changing ProjectStore\x3b\x0a- changing UI/session implementation\x3b\x0a- changing host configuration\x3b\x0a- changing USB runtime files\x3b\x0a- changing NUKRAX web files\x3b\x0a- changing recovery media.\x0a\x0aAny future implementation SHALL first inspect the completed LANUX architecture and existing tests.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 102. SOURCE OF TRUTH\x0a\x0aThe completed LANUX repository remains authoritative for actual identity, authentication, session, permission, and access-control implementation details.\x0a\x0aThis contract defines the portable USB architecture boundary and intended requirements.\x0a\x0aWhere this contract and the completed implementation differ, the discrepancy SHALL be explicitly identified and resolved before production implementation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 103. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CONDITION\x0a\x0aThe Identity, Session & Access Control architecture SHALL be considered complete only when:\x0a\x0a- creator identity is distinct from all other identities\x3b\x0a- authentication and authorization remain separate\x3b\x0a- sessions have explicit lifecycle\x3b\x0a- authorization is contextual\x3b\x0a- least privilege is enforced\x3b\x0a- critical operations require appropriate authorization\x3b\x0a- stale authorization is rejected\x3b\x0a- revocation propagates\x3b\x0a- USB identity does not authenticate the creator\x3b\x0a- host identity does not authenticate the creator\x3b\x0a- GitHub identity remains separate\x3b\x0a- provider identity remains separate\x3b\x0a- model identity remains separate\x3b\x0a- tool identity remains separate\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault remains authoritative for credentials\x3b\x0a- offline authentication is addressed\x3b\x0a- recovery access is bounded\x3b\x0a- security holds can invalidate authorization\x3b\x0a- session state is safely handled across restart\x3b\x0a- multi-session behavior is defined\x3b\x0a- delegated authority is bounded\x3b\x0a- physical USB copy is not treated as creator proof\x3b\x0a- compromised-host limitations are explicit\x3b\x0a- auditability is preserved\x3b\x0a- implementation requirements are verified from the completed LANUX system.\x0a\x0aUntil these conditions are satisfied, production identity/session/access-control implementation SHALL remain gated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 104. STATUS\x0a\x0aContract: IDENTITY, SESSION & ACCESS CONTROL CONTRACT\x0aNumber: 22\x0aStatus: LOCKED\x0aImplementation: NOT AUTHORIZED BY THIS CONTRACT\x0aProduction implementation: GATED ON FINAL LANUX REQUIREMENTS\x0a'@ | Set-Content -LiteralPath $Path -Encoding UTF8\x0a\x0aGet-Item -LiteralPath $Path | Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime;ef5fddfa-7684-4d37-8220-d78e4665db4e
